                'featured_image' => 'blog/blog-diamond-earrings.jpg',
                'excerpt'        => 'Diamond earrings are the ultimate versatile jewellery piece. From classic solitaire studs for daily wear to elaborate diamond jhumkas for weddings, this guide covers styles, pricing, and how to choose the perfect pair.',
                'content'        => '<h2>Why Diamond Earrings Are the Best First Diamond Purchase</h2><p>If you are buying your first piece of diamond jewellery, earrings are the smartest starting point. They are visible, versatile, and come in every price range. A pair of diamond studs works with a cotton kurti and a Kanjeevaram saree alike. They frame your face, catch the light beautifully, and unlike rings, you never have to take them off for daily tasks.</p><h2>Diamond Earring Styles Explained</h2><h3>Solitaire Studs (₹15,000 - ₹2,00,000)</h3><p>The most classic diamond earring. A single diamond per ear in a simple four-prong or bezel setting. Sizes range from 0.10 carat per ear (delicate, ₹15,000-₹25,000) to 0.50 carat per ear (statement, ₹1,00,000-₹2,00,000). Solitaire studs are the one-and-done earring — they work everywhere, always.</p><h3>Cluster Studs (₹20,000 - ₹80,000)</h3><p>Multiple small diamonds arranged to look like one large stone. This is the smart buyer\'s trick — a 7-stone cluster using 0.03-carat diamonds creates the visual impact of a 0.50-carat solitaire at a fraction of the price. Floral clusters, star shapes, and geometric patterns are popular choices.</p><h3>Diamond Drop Earrings (₹30,000 - ₹1,50,000)</h3><p>A diamond stud with a dangling element — usually a pear, marquise, or teardrop-shaped diamond or diamond cluster. Drop earrings add movement and drama while remaining elegant. Perfect for dinner parties, receptions, and festive occasions.</p><h3>Diamond Jhumkas (₹50,000 - ₹3,00,000)</h3><p>The fusion of Indian tradition and diamond luxury. Diamond-studded jhumkas replace traditional gold jhumkas with brilliant-cut or polki diamonds set in gold or platinum frameworks. They are bridal favourites and photograph spectacularly. Modern diamond jhumkas use lightweight hollow construction to keep weight manageable.</p><h3>Diamond Hoops (₹25,000 - ₹1,00,000)</h3><p>Small diamonds set along the curve of a hoop earring. Inside-out hoops (diamonds on both the front and inner face) sparkle from every angle. Diamond hoops are a Western-inspired style that pairs beautifully with contemporary Indian fashion — think diamond hoops with an Indo-Western gown or a sharara set.</p><h2>How to Choose the Right Diamond Earrings</h2><p><strong>For daily wear:</strong> Solitaire studs or small clusters in 18K gold. Secure screwback posts prevent loss. Budget ₹15,000-₹50,000.</p><p><strong>For work and social events:</strong> Diamond drops or medium hoops. Elegant enough to notice, subtle enough for professional settings. Budget ₹30,000-₹1,00,000.</p><p><strong>For weddings and bridal:</strong> Diamond jhumkas, chandbali-style diamond earrings, or large chandelier drops. These are your most-photographed accessories. Budget ₹1,00,000 and above.</p><h2>Diamond Earring Buying Tips for India</h2><ul><li><strong>Always ask for certification:</strong> IGI or GIA certificates confirm diamond quality. Avoid uncertified diamonds.</li><li><strong>Consider lab-grown diamonds:</strong> For earrings, lab-grown diamonds make exceptional sense — same sparkle, 60-80% lower price. Nobody can tell the difference when stones are on your ears.</li><li><strong>Check the setting:</strong> Bezel settings protect diamonds better for daily wear. Prong settings allow more light and sparkle for occasion wear.</li><li><strong>Match your skin tone:</strong> White gold and platinum enhance diamond brightness. Yellow gold adds warmth and pairs well with Indian skin tones.</li></ul><blockquote>Diamond earrings are the jewellery equivalent of a little black dress — essential, timeless, and always appropriate. Start with studs and let your collection grow from there. Shop diamond earrings at Trendimus.</blockquote>',
                'is_published'   => true,
                'published_at'   => now()->subDays(18),
                'view_count'     => 745,
