            [
                'title'          => 'Silver Anklets (Payal) for Women: Traditional & Modern Designs with Prices',
                'slug'           => 'silver-anklets-payal-traditional-modern-designs',
                'category'       => 'Anklets & Toe Rings',
                'tags'           => ['silver anklet', 'payal', 'silver payal designs', 'anklet for women', 'bridal anklet', 'ghungroo anklet', 'sterling silver anklet'],
                'featured_image' => 'blog/blog-silver-anklets.jpg',
                'excerpt'        => 'Silver anklets (payals) are one of India\'s most cherished jewellery traditions. From delicate chain anklets for daily wear to elaborate ghungroo payals for weddings, discover the best silver anklet designs and their prices.',
                'content'        => '<h2>The Silver Anklet: India\'s Most Musical Jewellery</h2><p>There is something magical about the gentle tinkling of silver anklets. Payals are deeply rooted in Indian culture — mentioned in ancient texts, celebrated in classical dance, and worn by brides across every Indian community. Today, silver anklets have evolved from purely traditional ornaments to fashionable accessories that complement both ethnic and Western outfits.</p><h2>Popular Silver Anklet Designs</h2><h3>Ghungroo Anklets (₹1,500 - ₹5,000)</h3><p>Tiny silver bells (ghungroos) strung on a silver chain. These produce the classic jingling sound associated with Indian anklets. Ghungroo anklets come in single-strand, double-strand, and elaborate multi-strand designs. The number of bells determines the sound intensity — 10-12 bells for a subtle tinkle, 20+ for a more pronounced ring.</p><h3>Chain Anklets (₹800 - ₹3,000)</h3><p>Simple sterling silver chains — box chain, Singapore twist, or figaro — worn around the ankle. These are the most popular choice for daily wear. Minimalist, comfortable, and versatile. Add a small charm pendant (heart, leaf, Om) for personalisation.</p><h3>Traditional Broad Anklets (₹3,000 - ₹12,000)</h3><p>Heavy, ornate anklets featuring intricate silver work — peacock motifs, floral patterns, temple designs, or geometric lattice work. These are 30-80 grams per anklet and are reserved for weddings, festivals, and special occasions. They are statement pieces that draw attention to henna-decorated feet.</p><h3>Charm Anklets (₹1,000 - ₹4,000)</h3><p>A chain anklet with dangling charms like hearts, stars, moons, evil eyes, or elephants. These have a Western bohemian influence and are popular with younger women. They look stunning with open sandals, heels, and barefoot beach looks.</p><h3>Beaded Anklets (₹600 - ₹2,500)</h3><p>Silver beads interspersed with coloured glass beads, turquoise, coral, or pearls. Beaded anklets have a relaxed, artisanal vibe. They are popular in Rajasthani and tribal-inspired designs.</p><h2>Anklet Sizing Guide</h2><p>To find your anklet size:</p><ol><li>Wrap a string or measuring tape around your ankle bone</li><li>Note the measurement in inches</li><li>Add 0.5-1 inch for a comfortable, slightly loose fit</li></ol><p>Standard women\'s anklet sizes: 9-10.5 inches. Most anklets come with an extension chain (1-2 inches) for adjustability. If you are between sizes, always go larger — an anklet that is too tight is uncomfortable and unflattering.</p><h2>Silver Anklets in Indian Traditions</h2><p>Anklets hold deep cultural significance across India:</p><ul><li><strong>North India:</strong> Brides wear heavy silver payals as part of solah shringar (16 adornments). Married women traditionally wear anklets as a symbol of their marital status.</li><li><strong>South India:</strong> Kolusu (anklets) are an essential part of bridal jewellery. Metti (toe rings) are often worn alongside anklets after marriage.</li><li><strong>Rajasthan:</strong> Thick, heavily decorated silver anklets called Pajeb are a mark of the Rajasthani bride. Some weigh over 200 grams per piece.</li><li><strong>Classical Dance:</strong> Kathak, Bharatanatyam, and other classical dancers wear ghungroo anklets with 50-200 bells for rhythmic accompaniment.</li></ul><h2>Caring for Silver Anklets</h2><p>Anklets face more wear than other jewellery because of their position:</p><ul><li>Remove before bathing — soap and water accelerate tarnishing</li><li>Dry thoroughly if they get wet from rain or puddles</li><li>Polish with a silver cloth weekly for daily-wear anklets</li><li>Store in anti-tarnish pouches when not wearing</li><li>Check clasp security regularly to prevent loss</li></ul><blockquote>A silver anklet adds grace, tradition, and a touch of music to every step. Whether you choose a simple chain or an elaborate ghungroo payal, it is a jewellery piece that connects you to centuries of Indian heritage. Browse our anklet collection at Trendimus.</blockquote>',
                'is_published'   => true,
                'published_at'   => now()->subDays(12),
                'view_count'     => 823,
                'seo_data'       => [
                    'meta_title'       => 'Silver Anklets (Payal) Designs & Prices in India | Trendimus',
                    'meta_description' => 'Explore silver anklet (payal) designs — ghungroo, chain, traditional & charm styles. Sizing guide, cultural significance & prices. Sterling silver anklets in India.',
