            // ============================================================
            [
                'title'          => 'Complete Bridal Jewellery Set Guide: What Every Indian Bride Needs in 2026',
                'slug'           => 'complete-bridal-jewellery-set-guide-india',
                'category'       => 'Bridal Sets',
                'tags'           => ['bridal jewellery', 'bridal jewellery set', 'Indian bridal jewellery', 'wedding jewellery', 'bridal set price', 'kundan bridal set', 'polki bridal jewellery'],
                'featured_image' => 'blog/blog-bridal-set.jpg',
                'excerpt'        => 'Planning your bridal jewellery can be overwhelming. This complete guide covers every piece an Indian bride needs — from the maang tikka to the payal — with budget breakdowns, style options, and shopping tips for 2026.',
                'content'        => '<h2>The Indian Bridal Jewellery Checklist</h2><p>An Indian bride\'s jewellery is not just adornment — it is an integral part of the wedding ceremony, carrying cultural, religious, and personal significance. The traditional solah shringar (16 adornments) prescribes specific jewellery for every part of the body. While modern brides adapt this to their personal style, the core pieces remain essential. Here is your complete guide.</p><h2>The Essential Bridal Jewellery Pieces</h2><h3>1. Maang Tikka / Matha Patti (₹5,000 - ₹50,000)</h3><p>Worn at the centre parting of the hair, the maang tikka is one of the most photographed bridal pieces. Simple tikkas feature a single pendant, while elaborate matha pattis extend across the forehead with chains. Kundan, polki, and pearl maang tikkas are the most popular choices. Tip: Choose a design that complements your necklace — they will be seen together in most photographs.</p><h3>2. Nath / Nose Ring (₹3,000 - ₹30,000)</h3><p>A bridal nath ranges from a small diamond nose pin to a large hoop with pearl chain extending to the ear. Maharashtrian brides traditionally wear a left-side nath, while Rajasthani naths are larger with coloured stones. The nath adds drama and frames the face beautifully in bridal portraits.</p><h3>3. Necklace / Haar (₹50,000 - ₹5,00,000+)</h3><p>Usually the most expensive piece in the bridal set. Most brides wear two necklaces — a choker (sitting at the base of the neck) and a longer rani haar (reaching the chest). Kundan polki sets give a traditional North Indian look, while temple jewellery suits South Indian brides. Diamond necklaces are chosen by brides wanting a contemporary aesthetic.</p><h3>4. Earrings / Jhumkas (₹15,000 - ₹1,00,000)</h3><p>Bridal earrings must match the necklace set and be comfortable for an 8-12 hour wedding day. Chandbalis, jhumkas with ear chains (sahara), and long chandelier earrings are classic choices. Lightweight options with hollow construction prevent earlobe fatigue during long ceremonies.</p><h3>5. Bangles / Chooda (₹20,000 - ₹2,00,000)</h3><p>Punjabi brides wear a red and white chooda, while others choose gold, diamond, or kundan bangles. A set of matching bangles on both wrists completes the bridal look. Many brides now mix traditional bangles with a diamond bracelet or watch on one wrist for a modern twist.</p><h3>6. Haath Phool / Hand Harness (₹5,000 - ₹40,000)</h3><p>A chain connecting a ring to a bracelet, draping across the back of the hand. Haath phool adds intricate detail to mehndi-covered hands and photographs beautifully during ring exchange and vidaai moments.</p><h3>7. Kamarband / Waist Belt (₹10,000 - ₹80,000)</h3><p>A jewelled belt worn at the waist over the bridal lehenga or saree. Kamarbands accentuate the waistline and add a regal element. They are particularly stunning in polki, kundan, or pearl designs.</p><h3>8. Anklets / Payal (₹3,000 - ₹15,000)</h3><p>Silver anklets with ghungroo bells complete the bridal look from head to toe. The tinkling sound of bridal payals is an iconic part of Indian weddings.</p><h2>Bridal Jewellery Budget Planning</h2><p>A realistic bridal jewellery budget in India for 2026:</p><ul><li><strong>Budget bridal set (artificial/imitation):</strong> ₹15,000 - ₹50,000 — Beautiful for photographs, no gold investment</li><li><strong>Mid-range bridal set (gold-plated kundan/polki):</strong> ₹50,000 - ₹2,00,000 — Real craftsmanship, lighter on the wallet</li><li><strong>Premium bridal set (22K gold with stones):</strong> ₹3,00,000 - ₹10,00,000 — Heirloom quality, retains gold value</li><li><strong>Luxury bridal set (diamond and gold):</strong> ₹10,00,000+ — Timeless investment pieces</li></ul><h2>Shopping Tips for Bridal Jewellery</h2><ul><li>Start shopping 3-4 months before the wedding for custom pieces</li><li>Try jewellery with your actual bridal outfit — colours and proportions change everything</li><li>Photograph yourself wearing the jewellery from the angles you will be photographed at the wedding</li><li>Ensure all gold jewellery has BIS hallmark certification</li><li>Keep all receipts, certificates, and warranty cards organised</li><li>Consider renting heavy pieces you will only wear once</li></ul><blockquote>Your bridal jewellery tells your story — your heritage, your style, and the new chapter you are beginning. Plan thoughtfully, shop early, and choose pieces that make you feel like the most beautiful version of yourself. Explore our bridal collections at Trendimus.</blockquote>',
                'is_published'   => true,
                'published_at'   => now()->subDays(10),
                'view_count'     => 1567,
                'seo_data'       => [
                    'meta_title'       => 'Complete Bridal Jewellery Set Guide for Indian Brides (2026) | Trendimus',
                    'meta_description' => 'Everything an Indian bride needs — maang tikka, necklace, earrings, bangles, haath phool & more. Budget breakdowns, style options & shopping tips for bridal jewellery.',
                ],
            ],
            [
                'title'          => 'Mangalsutra Designs 2026: Modern, Diamond & Traditional Styles with Prices',
                'slug'           => 'mangalsutra-designs-modern-diamond-traditional',
                'category'       => 'Necklaces & Pendants',
                'tags'           => ['mangalsutra', 'mangalsutra designs', 'diamond mangalsutra', 'modern mangalsutra', 'gold mangalsutra', 'mangalsutra price', 'short mangalsutra', 'daily wear mangalsutra'],
                'featured_image' => 'blog/blog-mangalsutra.jpg',
                'excerpt'        => 'The mangalsutra has transformed from a purely traditional ornament to a modern fashion accessory. Discover the latest mangalsutra designs for 2026 — from sleek diamond pendants on black bead chains to contemporary gold designs perfect for everyday wear.',
                'content'        => '<h2>The Mangalsutra: Tradition Meets Modern Style</h2><p>The mangalsutra is one of the most significant pieces of Indian jewellery — symbolising the sacred bond of marriage. Traditionally a long gold chain with black beads and a pendant, the modern mangalsutra has evolved dramatically. Today\'s married women want a mangalsutra that honours tradition while fitting seamlessly into their professional and social lives. The result is a beautiful range of contemporary designs that bridge both worlds.</p><h2>Types of Mangalsutra Designs</h2><h3>Traditional Long Mangalsutra (₹30,000 - ₹1,50,000)</h3><p>The classic 24-30 inch mangalsutra with two strings of black and gold beads meeting at a gold pendant. The pendant design varies by region — Maharashtrian brides wear a vati pendant (two cups), South Indian brides wear the thali on a yellow thread, and North Indian versions feature various auspicious motifs. These are worn during the wedding ceremony and on special occasions.</p><h3>Short Diamond Mangalsutra (₹20,000 - ₹1,00,000)</h3><p>The most popular modern mangalsutra style. A 16-18 inch black bead chain with a diamond-studded pendant in 18K gold. The pendant is small, elegant, and looks like a fashionable necklace. This is what most working women wear daily — it is subtle enough for the office, stylish enough for social gatherings, and meaningful enough for tradition.</p><h3>Bracelet Mangalsutra (₹15,000 - ₹50,000)</h3><p>A revolutionary concept — the mangalsutra worn on the wrist instead of the neck. A delicate black bead and gold chain bracelet with a small diamond pendant. Perfect for women who prefer not to wear neckpieces daily but want to keep the mangalsutra tradition. This design has become hugely popular in metro cities.</p><h3>Pendant-Only Mangalsutra (₹10,000 - ₹60,000)</h3><p>Just the diamond or gold pendant, designed to be worn on any chain. Some women switch between a black bead chain for traditional occasions and a plain gold chain for everyday wear. The pendant is the focus — modern designs include geometric shapes, infinity symbols, evil eye, and abstract floral patterns.</p><h2>Regional Mangalsutra Traditions</h2><ul><li><strong>Maharashtra:</strong> Two vati (cup-shaped) pendants on black and gold beads. Traditionally 30 inches long.</li><li><strong>South India (Thali):</strong> Gold pendant on a yellow sacred thread, later replaced with a gold chain. Designs vary by community — Tamilian, Kannadiga, Malayali each have distinct patterns.</li><li><strong>North India:</strong> Black bead chain with a gold or diamond pendant. More standardised design.</li><li><strong>Bengali:</strong> Not traditionally a mangalsutra culture, but modern Bengali brides increasingly adopt diamond mangalsutra designs.</li></ul><h2>How to Choose a Daily-Wear Mangalsutra</h2><p><strong>Length:</strong> 16-18 inches sits beautifully at the collarbone and stays inside most necklines when needed.<br><strong>Pendant size:</strong> 10-15mm diameter is ideal — visible but not overpowering.<br><strong>Metal:</strong> 18K gold for durability. The diamond pendant should be certified (IGI or GIA).<br><strong>Chain:</strong> Machine-made black bead chain is more durable than hand-strung. Look for secure spring-ring or lobster clasps.<br><strong>Weight:</strong> Under 8 grams total for comfort during all-day wear.</p><h2>Mangalsutra as an Investment</h2><p>Unlike fashion jewellery, a well-chosen mangalsutra retains value:</p><ul><li>Gold content holds its value and appreciates over time</li><li>Diamonds retain value, especially certified stones above 0.20 carats</li><li>Quality mangalsutras last a lifetime with minimal maintenance</li><li>Many jewellers offer exchange and upgrade programmes</li></ul><blockquote>The modern mangalsutra proves that tradition and style are not opposites. Choose a design that honours your bond while expressing your personality. Browse our mangalsutra collection at Trendimus — from classic to contemporary.</blockquote>',
                'is_published'   => true,
                'published_at'   => now()->subDays(8),
